.TH CLOUD-BREAK 1
.SH NAME
cloud-break \- build and process evaporating clouds
.SH USAGE
.nf
cloud-break - build and process evaporating clouds

Usage:
  cloud-break [options]                    open the library (needs a terminal)
  cloud-break <command> [options]

Commands:
  list                                     clouds in the library
  show <cloud>                             one cloud in full
  render <cloud>                           the cloud as a diagram
  check [<cloud>...]                       validate clouds
  new                                      create a cloud
  set <cloud> <slot> <text>                write a statement
  title <cloud> <text>                     rename a cloud
  parties <cloud> <first> <second>         name the two sides
  assume add <cloud> <arrow> <text>        record an assumption
  assume set <cloud> <id> <text>           reword an assumption
  assume verdict <cloud> <id> <word>       judge an assumption
  assume evidence <cloud> <id> <text>      record the evidence
  assume rm <cloud> <id>                   delete an assumption
  inject add <cloud> [--breaks ids] <text> record an injection
  inject rm <cloud> <id>                   delete an injection
  verify <cloud> [--undo]                  record that the cloud evaporates
  delete <cloud>                           remove a cloud
  export <cloud>                           print the whole document
  version                                  print the version
  help [topic]                             print help

Options:
  --dir dir      library directory (default $CLOUD_BREAK_HOME, then
                 $XDG_DATA_HOME/cloud-break, then ~/.local/share/cloud-break)
  --demo         work on a throwaway library holding the example clouds; it is
                 thrown away with the run, so it cannot be combined with --dir
  --json         print machine-readable JSON
  -h, --help     print this help and exit; --help <topic> for the topics below
  --version      print the version and exit
  --man          print the manual page as roff and exit

  A flag may appear before or after the command name.

Naming:
  <cloud> names a cloud by its id, or by any unambiguous prefix of it, so the
  first few characters are enough. <id> names an assumption or an injection the
  same way. list and show print the ids; nothing has to be guessed.

  A missing id fails; an ambiguous one fails and names the candidates, so a
  short prefix can never silently pick the wrong record.

Text:
  Every free-text operand runs to the end of the command line, so quotes are
  optional: "cloud-break set abc A ship on time" and
  "cloud-break set abc A 'ship on time'" mean the same thing. Pass an empty
  string to clear a statement or an evidence field.

Reading:
  list prints one line per cloud:

    <id> <status> <filled>/<total> <assumptions> <updated> <title>

  The title comes last because it is the only field that may contain spaces.

  show prints labelled sections: the statements, the assumptions with their
  verdicts, the injections, the five process steps and the validation issues.

  check prints one line per finding and nothing at all when a cloud is clean:

    <cloud> <severity> <message>

  Severity is error, warning or info. Only an error fails the run.

  render prints the diagram. It takes --width (default 74, minimum 44). Piped
  output carries no escape sequences, so it can be read or diffed as it is.

Writing:
  A command that changes something prints the id it acted on, one line, and
  nothing else. So the next command can be built from this one:

    id=$(cloud-break new --title "Release scope")
    cloud-break set "$id" A "ship on time"

  Commands that create a record print the new id: new, assume add, inject add.
  Commands that change one print the id of the record they changed. delete
  prints the id of the cloud it removed. verify refuses until an injection
  breaks an invalid assumption, because a cloud is never marked dissolved on
  your behalf; --undo withdraws the verification.

JSON:
  --json replaces the text shapes. A read command prints an object or an array
  of objects with every field always present, so "absent" never has to be told
  apart from "empty":

  list   [{id, title, status, progress{filled,total}, assumptions, verified,
           updated}]
  show   one object: id, title, parties, status, progress, statements (all five
         slots), assumptions, injections, verified, created, updated, steps,
         issues. An assumption carries its verdict, its evidence and the
         injections that break it; an injection carries the assumptions it
         breaks.
  check  [{cloud, severity, message}]
  export the stored cloud document, the same shape "new --from" reads

  A write command prints {kind, cloud, id}, where kind is cloud, assumption or
  injection and id is the record it acted on. delete adds "deleted": true.
  render always prints text; a diagram is not a record.

Exit status:
  0    the command ran, and no check found an error
  1    the command failed (a missing cloud, an unreadable document, a failed
       write), or check found an error
  2    the command line cannot be used: an unknown command, a missing or
       ambiguous operand, an unknown slot, arrow or verdict, a bad flag
  130  the command was interrupted

Environment:
  CLOUD_BREAK_HOME  library directory, used when --dir is absent
  XDG_DATA_HOME     library directory is $XDG_DATA_HOME/cloud-break

Examples:
  cloud-break                                   open the library
  cloud-break --demo                            browse the example clouds
  cloud-break new --title "Release scope" --parties "Eng,Sales"
  cloud-break list --status draft
  cloud-break set 4f2a1c A "ship on time"
  cloud-break assume add 4f2a1c B-D "freezing scope prevents regressions"
  cloud-break assume verdict 4f2a1c 9c8b7a invalid --evidence "we hotfixed twice"
  cloud-break inject add 4f2a1c --breaks 9c8b7a "cut scope to a fixed date"
  cloud-break verify 4f2a1c
  cloud-break show 4f2a1c --json
  cloud-break check
  cloud-break export 4f2a1c | cloud-break --dir ./copy new --from -

Help topics:
  cloud-break --help clouds   the five statements, the arrows, and the words
                              the commands accept
  cloud-break --help agent    driving cloud-break from a script or an agent
.fi
.SH CLOUDS
.nf
The cloud

  An evaporating cloud states a conflict as five statements:

    A   the objective both sides share
    B   a need of the first side          C   a need of the second side
    D   what the first side wants         D'  what the second side wants

  A-B, A-C, B-D and C-D' are necessity arrows: "in order to <upper>, we must
  <lower>". D and D' collide, and that collision is the conflict.

  A cloud is only worth working on when all five statements are filled in and D
  and D' actually disagree. check reports an empty statement as an error, and a
  D' that only negates D as info.

Statements:
  set accepts the slot names A, B, C, D and D'. D2 is accepted for D', because
  a bare apostrophe is awkward to quote in a shell. An empty text clears the
  statement. Statements are collapsed to one line.

Arrows:
  assume add accepts A-B, A-C, B-D, C-D' and D-D'. The hyphen is optional and
  D2 stands in for D', so C-D', C-D2 and CD2 all name the same arrow.

  D-D' carries the conflict itself: assumptions on it are the reasons the two
  wants cannot both be satisfied.

Assumptions:
  An assumption is a "because" behind an arrow: the unstated claim the arrow
  depends on. Every arrow hides some, and the cloud evaporates when an
  assumption is invalidated.

  An assumption has a text, a verdict and optional evidence:

  verdict       unassessed  not judged yet (the default)
                holds       there is cause and evidence for it
                questionable  plausible, but not established
                invalid     it does not hold

  assume verdict also accepts unknown or none for unassessed, hold or true for
  holds, question for questionable, and false for invalid.

  The verdict is a judgement about the assumption, not about the person holding
  it. Mark one invalid when you can show it does not hold, and say what breaks
  it in the evidence field.

Injections:
  An injection is the change that makes an assumption irrelevant: the
  breakthrough that dissolves the conflict instead of trading one want against
  the other. An injection names the assumptions it breaks, so the link is
  recorded from both ends.

Process:
  The five stages are derived from the cloud itself, so no stage has to be
  ticked off by hand. show and check report where a cloud stands:

  1  State the conflict           all five statements are filled in
  2  Surface the assumptions      every arrow has at least one assumption
  3  Find the weak link           an assumption has been judged invalid
  4  Inject a breakthrough        an injection breaks that assumption
  5  Verify the cloud evaporates  you confirmed the injection dissolves it

Status:
  A cloud's status follows the same evidence:

  draft         the statements are not complete
  open          complete, and nothing has fallen yet
  dissolving    an assumption is invalid, but no verified injection breaks it
  dissolved     an injection breaks an invalid assumption, and you verified it

  Nothing is marked dissolved on your behalf: verify is the step where a person
  says the conflict is gone, and verify --undo takes it back.

Validation:
  check reports three severities. error blocks: a missing statement, D and D'
  saying the same thing, an arrow joining an empty statement. warning means the
  cloud is inconclusive: an arrow with no assumptions, an injection that breaks
  nothing or breaks an assumption judged to hold, an injection pointing at an
  assumption that no longer exists, blank text. info is a hint: D' only negates
  D, an invalid assumption nothing injects against yet.
.fi
.SH AGENT
.nf
Driving cloud-break

  cloud-break is built so a script or an agent can run it without asking. This
  topic is the contract, then a worked sequence, then how to use it well.

The contract:
  stdout   data only: ids, records, diagrams
  stderr   every diagnostic, prefixed "cloud-break:"
  status   0 success, 1 failed or a check found an error, 2 the command line
           cannot be used, 130 interrupted
  --json   one object or array, with every field always present

  A writing command prints the id it acted on, one line, so this works:

    id=$(cloud-break new --title "Release scope")
    asm=$(cloud-break assume add "$id" B-D "freezing scope prevents regressions")

  Reference a cloud, an assumption or an injection by its id or by any
  unambiguous prefix. Never guess an id: list and show print them. A prefix
  that matches nothing exits 1; one that matches several exits 2 and names the
  candidates, so a wrong guess cannot corrupt the wrong cloud.

A whole cloud, in order:

  # 1. create it, with the two sides named
  id=$(cloud-break new --title "Release scope" --parties "Eng,Sales")

  # 2. state all five statements
  cloud-break set "$id" A "ship on time"
  cloud-break set "$id" B "no regressions"      # Eng's need
  cloud-break set "$id" C "promised features"   # Sales's need
  cloud-break set "$id" D "freeze the scope"
  cloud-break set "$id" D2 "add every feature"

  # 3. surface the assumptions, one per arrow to start
  cloud-break assume add "$id" A-B "shipping on time is what the objective means"
  cloud-break assume add "$id" A-C "features are what the objective means"
  cloud-break assume add "$id" B-D "scope cannot change without regressions"
  cloud-break assume add "$id" C-D2 "every promised feature must ship"
  cloud-break assume add "$id" D-D2 "the date and the feature list are fixed"

  # 4. challenge them; record the evidence with the verdict
  weak=$(cloud-break assume add "$id" B-D "a regression is only a regression against a promise")
  cloud-break assume verdict "$id" "$weak" invalid \\
    --evidence "two hotfixes shipped last month without freezing scope"

  # 5. inject against the assumption you invalidated
  cloud-break inject add "$id" --breaks "$weak" "cut scope to a fixed date, keep the date"

  # 6. check, then verify
  cloud-break check "$id"
  cloud-break verify "$id"
  cloud-break show "$id"

Reading it back:
  cloud-break list --status dissolving
  cloud-break show "$id" --json | jq '.assumptions[] | select(.verdict=="invalid")'
  cloud-break render "$id"
  cloud-break export "$id" > cloud.json
  cloud-break --dir ./scratch new --from cloud.json

Working from a scratch library:
  --dir keeps a job out of the user's real library, and --demo gives a throwaway
  library holding the example clouds, so those can be read without being
  touched:

    cloud-break --dir ./clouds new --title "..."
    cloud-break --demo list
    cloud-break --demo show <id>

  A cloud document is one JSON file named after its id, so a whole library can
  be inspected, copied or diffed with ordinary file tools.

How to use it well:
  - Ask before inventing. A and the two needs come from the people in the
    conflict, not from what seems likely.
  - One assumption per claim. "Scope cannot change and nothing can regress"
    is two assumptions; record them separately so each can be judged.
  - Judge the assumption, not the person. Invalid means "this does not hold",
    and the evidence field is where you say why.
  - An injection must break an assumption you judged invalid. An injection
    that merely splits the difference leaves the conflict in place.
  - Do not verify on the user's behalf. verify records that a person checked
    the injection dissolves the conflict; run it only when they say so, and
    use verify --undo to take it back.
  - check is cheap. Run it after the statements and again after the
    assumptions; an error means the cloud cannot be worked on at all.

  The text output is line-oriented and greppable: the id is the first field,
  and the only field that may contain spaces is the last. Prefer --json when
  reading more than one field; prefer the text shape when scanning.
.fi
